.\" renesis.1 -- manual page for renesis(1)
.\" Render with:  man ./renesis.1
.\" Plain text:   groff -man -Tutf8 renesis.1 | col -bx > renesis.1.txt
.TH RENESIS 1 "August 2026" "Renesis v92.4" "User Commands"
.
.SH NAME
renesis \- energy\-aware logic synthesis for adiabatic and reversible
technologies
.
.SH SYNOPSIS
The C engine. This is the fast one, and it is what most runs should use.
.PP
.B csrc/renesis
.RI [ options ]
.I netlist
.br
.B csrc/renesis \-\-convert
.I out.fmt
.RI [ options ]
.I netlist
.br
.B csrc/renesis
.BR \-\-list\-tech | \-\-show\-options | \-\-help
.PP
The Python driver. Slower, and it owns the surfaces the engine does not have.
.B PYTHONHASHSEED=0
is a determinism requirement and the driver refuses to start without it; the
.B renesis
wrapper script sets it, so export it only when calling the module directly.
.PP
.B export PYTHONHASHSEED=0
.br
.B renesis
.RI [ options ]
.I netlist
.br
.B python3 scripts_adiabatic/renesis.py
.RI [ options ]
.I netlist
.br
.B renesis \-\-convert
.I out.fmt
.RI [ options ]
.I netlist
.br
.B renesis analyze \-\-relation
.I file
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B renesis ui
.br
.B renesis
.BR \-\-list\-tech | \-\-show\-options | \-\-help
.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B renesis
maps a combinational netlist onto a target technology and reports the energy
the mapped network dissipates per cycle. It is aimed at families whose cost
is not counted in gates: adiabatic and reversible logic, where the charge
returned to the power clock, the series depth of a conducting path and the
number of buffer stages decide the result.
.PP
The flow is a fixed sequence of stages:
.PP
.RS 4
parse \[->] [netprep] \[->] [re\-synthesis] \[->] tag sweep \[->] cover and map
\[->] buffer insertion \[->] energy report
.RE
.PP
Two energy tables are reported for every run.
.B T1
is the uncapped dissipation of the mapped network.
.B T2
is the dissipation after the series bound of the target family has been
enforced by buffer insertion. Every optimization in the tool is gated on
both: a candidate is accepted only when it improves one table and worsens
neither. A pass can therefore consume runtime and hand the netlist back
unchanged, and that outcome is reported rather than hidden.
.PP
Two front ends exist and are kept in agreement by a byte\-level contract. The
orchestration driver
.RB ( renesis ,
a shell wrapper around
.IR scripts_adiabatic/renesis.py )
sequences the stages and owns the multi\-run surfaces. The C engine
.RI ( csrc/renesis )
implements the stages and is roughly an order of magnitude faster. Both read
the same option table and the same technology description, so a setting means
the same thing on either. Options that only the driver can honour are refused
by name in the engine rather than silently ignored; see
.B PARITY BOUNDARY
below.
.PP
Every run is deterministic.
.B PYTHONHASHSEED=0
is required by the driver and asserted at startup.
.
.SH ACCEPTED INPUT
Verilog
.RB ( .v ),
ISCAS
.RB ( .isc ),
PLA
.RB ( .pla ),
AIGER
.RB ( .aig ,
.BR .aag ),
ISCAS\-89 bench
.RB ( .bench )
and BLIF
.RB ( .blif ).
The driver accepts all of these; the engine accepts
.BR .v ,
.BR .isc ,
.BR .pla ,
.B .aig
and
.BR .aag .
.
.SH OPTIONS
Every option in the table can be set two ways: with its dedicated flag where
one exists, or with
.BI \-\-option " NAME" = VALUE
for any option at all. The complete table, with defaults and a description of
each, is in
.IR RENESIS\-OPTIONS\-LIST.md ,
which gives it twice: once as the engine accepts it and once as the driver
accepts it. The options most runs need are listed here.
.PP
Most of the dedicated flags below exist on the driver only. On the engine,
use the
.BI \-\-option " NAME" = VALUE
form instead; see
.B PARITY BOUNDARY
for the exact split.
.
.SS Target
.TP
.BI \-\-tech " name"
Target technology, read from
.IR config/technology/ name .json .
Default
.BR tgate_sl6 .
.TP
.B \-\-list\-tech
List the available technologies and exit.
.TP
.BI \-\-tech\-dir " dir"
Read technology descriptions from
.I dir
instead of the shipped directory.
.
.SS Covering and mapping
.TP
.BI \-\-k " n"
Cut size for the K\-feasible cover, 4 to 16. Default 12.
.TP
.BI \-\-cover " mode"
.B tech
(default) prices a cover against the technology cost model;
.B switching
prices it against estimated switching activity.
.TP
.BI \-\-route " mode"
.BR auto (default),
.B shallow
or
.BR structural .
.TP
.BI \-\-cap " n"
Buffer\-insertion cap: the longest series chain permitted before a buffer is
inserted. Defaults to the target family's own series limit.
.TP
.BI \-\-tag\-trials " n"
Monte\-Carlo trials in the activity sweep. Default 4000.
.
.SS Re\-synthesis passes
All are off unless asked for, because a default run is meant to be fast.
.TP
.B \-\-netprep
Structural preprocessing before covering: hashing, balancing and local
rewriting.
.TP
.B \-\-davio
Affine\-cut (Davio) extraction: a cut that is affine in every variable is
re\-emitted as an XOR tree.
.TP
.BI \-\-elim " mode"
Bounded elimination and algebraic extraction:
.BR none (default),
.B single
or
.BR both .
The pre\-v89.2 spelling
.B \-\-factor
still works.
.TP
.B \-\-prefix
Parallel\-prefix re\-synthesis of adder\-like structure.
.TP
.B \-\-linwin ", " \-\-mowin
Interior affine\-window re\-synthesis, single\- and multi\-output.
.TP
.B \-\-bdec
Linear pre\-filter: a global affine re\-encoding of the output space, realized
as a decoder network at the boundary. See
.B THE LINEAR PRE\-FILTER
below.
.TP
.B \-\-optimize\-all
Enable
.BR \-\-davio ,
.BR \-\-prefix ,
.B \-\-linwin
and
.BR \-\-mowin .
Not
.BR \-\-bdec .
.TP
.BI \-\-passes " n"
Re\-window passes per optimization, or a per\-pass map such as
.BR davio=3,elim=1 .
.TP
.BI \-\-price\-cap " n"
Candidates priced per pass. Default 800.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-prescreen
Turn off the pre-flight screen. By default, before a pass searches, a cheap
structural test asks whether the pass could construct any candidate at all on
this netlist; when it could not, the pass reports a screened verdict naming
the condition that failed instead of searching. The screen is a necessary
condition on candidate existence, so the mapped result is the same either way
and only the runtime differs. On the engine, use
.BR \-\-option " " prescreen=false .
.PP
These passes are not free. On a 518\-gate circuit a default run takes tens of
seconds,
.B \-\-davio
adds tens of seconds more, and
.B \-\-linwin
can add several minutes.
.IR RENESIS\-MANUAL.md
carries the measured table.
.
.SS Cut\-size ladder
.TP
.BI \-\-k\-ladder " list"
Run the covering stage at each K in
.IR list ,
for example
.BR 12,8,6,4 .
The first rung is the incumbent and always completes; later rungs are priced
and accepted only under the acceptance rule, so the ladder cannot return
something worse than its first rung. Empty (the default) means off.
.TP
.BI \-\-k\-ladder\-s " s"
Wall\-clock budget in seconds for the rungs after the first. 0 means no
budget.
.TP
.BI \-\-accept " rule"
Rung acceptance:
.B both
(default) requires an improvement on one energy table and no regression on
the other;
.B t2
accepts on a capped\-table improvement alone. Applies to the ladder only.
.
.SS Output
.TP
.BI \-o " base"
Write the result as
.IR base .*:
the technology\-independent netlist and its statistics, and the
technology\-mapped netlist and its statistics.
.TP
.BI \-\-out\-format " fmt"
Format for the independent netlist. Default
.BR blif .
.TP
.BI \-\-verilog\-style " style"
.BR cells (default),
.B iscas
or
.BR assign .
.TP
.BI \-\-json " file"
Write the full run record, including every non\-default option and every
pass verdict, as JSON.
.TP
.BI \-\-schematic " base"
Write visualization files:
.IB base _independent.dot
(Graphviz),
.IB base _independent.json
(Yosys JSON, which
.B netlistsvg
renders as a gate\-level schematic) and
.IB base _mapped.dot
with blocks colored by power\-clock phase. Matching
.B .svg
files are written when
.B dot
or
.B netlistsvg
are on
.BR PATH .
.TP
.BI \-\-spice\-gen " base"
Write
.IB base .sp ,
an ngspice deck of the mapped network. The device models are stubs, so a
transient validates function and the clocking discipline, not the tool's
energy figures.
.
.SS Drive model
.TP
.BI \-\-pi\-drive " name"
.B uniform
(default),
.B saif
or
.BR transition\-relation .
The drive model is recorded on every figure, because the activity tags feed
the cover: a workload\-driven result is a different circuit, not the same
circuit measured differently.
.TP
.BI \-\-saif " file"
Read per\-input probability and activity from a SAIF file.
.TP
.BI \-\-saif\-cycles " n" ", " \-\-saif\-period " p"
Convert SAIF toggle counts to per\-cycle activity, either directly or by
dividing the duration by
.IR p .
.
.SS General
.TP
.BI \-\-option " NAME" = VALUE
Set any option from the table. Repeatable.
.TP
.BI \-\-options " file"
Use a different option table.
.TP
.BI \-\-wall\-s " s"
Wall\-clock budget honoured by the search kits.
.TP
.B \-\-net\-activity
Measure per\-net toggle rates. Reported, never priced.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-check
Skip the converter and round\-trip checks.
.TP
.B \-\-show\-options
Print the resolved option set and exit. Use this to see exactly what a
command line resolves to before spending the runtime.
.TP
.B \-q
Quiet.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print usage and exit.
.
.SH MODES
Besides synthesis, the driver has three other modes.
.TP
.BI \-\-convert " out.fmt"
Translation only, no synthesis. The emitted file is re\-parsed and
equivalence\-checked against the input, so a converter that quietly renames or
drops something fails here rather than downstream.
.B \-\-no\-check
skips that.
Writes
.BR .blif ,
.BR .v ,
.B .bench
and
.BR .pla .
.TP
.B analyze
Treat a sequential machine given as a transition relation as a Markov chain
and report the reachable set, absorbing and dead\-end states, recurrent
classes, eccentricity from reset, and, when the structure permits it, the
stationary law and the per\-bit tags. A machine that halts gets its
precondition failure reported instead of an average.
.B renesis analyze \-\-help
for the full list.
.TP
.B ui
Start the synthesis server on a free port and open a browser. Ctrl\-C stops
it. The page's option panel is generated from the option table, so it cannot
disagree with the command line. On macOS,
.B renesis\-ui.command
does the same from Finder.
.
.SH THE LINEAR PRE\-FILTER
.B \-\-bdec
searches for an invertible matrix B over GF(2) that re\-encodes the output
space, maps the re\-encoded function, and realizes the inverse as a decoder
network at the boundary. It pays only where the circuit's outputs are affine
functions of the inputs whose supports overlap enough that a combination is
cheaper to build than the outputs are separately: syndrome formers, Gray and
binary code converters, parity trees and similar boundary logic. It is
correctly inert on circuits without that structure, including every circuit
in both shipped benchmark sets, and the run says so rather than reporting a
change it did not make. Tuned with
.BR \-\-option " " bdec_wmax ,
.B bdec_pool
and
.BR bdec_rounds .
.
.SH THE PRE\-FLIGHT SCREEN
Every optimization pass has an acceptance gate: a candidate is constructed,
equivalence\-checked, priced, and accepted only if it improves one energy
table and worsens neither. A screen is a NECESSARY CONDITION on candidate
existence, evaluated before the search: if no candidate can be constructed,
none can be accepted, so the pass would have returned the netlist unchanged.
Screening therefore changes how long an answer takes and not what the answer
is, which is why the release validation checks the two configurations against
each other and requires the mapped netlist to be byte\-identical.
.PP
Two passes carry one today.
.B \-\-prefix
re\-synthesizes a carry chain, so it reports
.RB \(dq "no carry chains at l_min=N" \(dq
when the netlist has none.
.B \-\-bdec
realizes a row of B only when the outputs it combines are structurally affine
and their supports cancel; since the search accepts one elementary row
addition at a time, the first accepted move can only produce a row of weight
two, so the screen asks whether any PAIR of affine outputs cancels. On the
ISCAS circuits the answer is no and the pass stops in about the time a default
run takes rather than searching for a minute or more.
.PP
A screened pass says so, and names the condition that failed, rather than
falling silent.
.
.SH PARITY BOUNDARY
The two front ends do not accept the same command lines, and the difference
is worth stating exactly, because the failure mode being avoided is a setting
that appears to be taken and is not.
.PP
The engine has a small flag set: the one given under
.B OPTIONS
as
.BR \-\-tech ,
.BR \-\-tech\-dir ,
.BR \-\-cap ,
.BR \-\-option ,
.BR \-\-options ,
.BR \-\-json ,
.BR \-o ,
.BR \-\-convert ,
.BR \-\-no\-check ,
.BR \-\-tags ,
.BR \-\-dump\-tags ,
.BR \-\-pi\-drive ,
.BR \-\-saif ,
.BR \-\-saif\-cycles ,
.BR \-\-saif\-period ,
.BR \-\-spice\-gen ,
.BR \-\-schematic ,
.BR \-\-bundle ,
.BR \-\-list\-tech ,
.BR \-\-show\-options ,
.B \-q
and
.BR \-h / \-\-help .
Every other dedicated flag in this page
.RB ( \-\-k ,
.BR \-\-cover ,
.BR \-\-davio ,
.BR \-\-elim ,
.BR \-\-k\-ladder ,
.BR \-\-passes ,
.BR \-\-optimize\-all
and the rest) is a driver spelling and is rejected by the engine as an
unknown flag.
.PP
Every setting those flags stand for is still reachable on the engine through
.BI \-\-option " NAME" = VALUE \fR,
and it is honoured there, including the K\-ladder, the pass order and the
wall\-clock budget. Only three names in the option table are not implemented
by the engine at all:
.BR family_for_pricing ,
.B emit_buffers
and
.BR no_emit_buffers .
Those three are refused by name rather than ignored, so a command line that
needs one of them fails instead of quietly producing a different result. A
run that needs them needs the driver.
.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The run completed and the result verified.
.TP
.B 1
A usage error, an unreadable input, or a refused option.
.TP
.B 2
An internal failure: a resource limit, a failed equivalence check, or a
consistency assertion that did not hold.
.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B PYTHONHASHSEED
Must be 0. The driver asserts it and exits if it is not; the
.B renesis
wrapper sets it for you.
.TP
.B CUDD_DIR
Where the CUDD decision\-diagram library is installed. Read at build time by
the makefiles, not at run time. Defaults to
.IR $HOME/opt/cudd .
.TP
.B ADSHIM
Path to the compiled decision\-diagram shim. Defaults to
.I tools/adshim/libadshim.so
under the bundle root.
.TP
.B RENESIS_ROOT
Bundle root for the engine, when it cannot be derived from
.IR argv[0] .
The
.B \-\-bundle
flag does the same thing.
.TP
.B ABC
Path to an ABC binary. Optional; when set, the browser interface adds a
baseline comparison column.
.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I config/renesis_options.json
The option table. Every default in this page comes from here, and both front
ends and the browser interface read it.
.TP
.IR config/technology/ *.json
Technology descriptions: cell model, structural constraints, clocking
discipline.
.TP
.I RENESIS\-OPTIONS\-LIST.md
Every option, its default, and what it does.
.TP
.I RENESIS\-MANUAL.md
The full manual, including the measured runtime table.
.TP
.I validate_all.sh
The release validation. Reproduces the published numbers end to end.
.TP
.I other_options_validate.sh
Exercises the options the release validation does not reach.
.PP
Relative paths on the command line resolve against the bundle root, not the
shell's working directory, because the driver changes directory to the bundle
so its data files are findable. Use absolute paths if that is inconvenient.
.
.SH EXAMPLES
Map a circuit onto the default target and report its energy:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
csrc/renesis csrc/samples/c17.v
.EE
.RE
.PP
The same, with a machine\-readable record:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
csrc/renesis \-\-json run.json csrc/samples/c17.v
.EE
.RE
.PP
Target a different family and write both netlists:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
csrc/renesis \-\-tech 2lal \-o build/c432 bench/c432.v
.EE
.RE
.PP
Turn on a re\-synthesis pass and a cut\-size ladder on the engine, where every
setting goes through \-\-option:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
csrc/renesis \-\-option davio=true \-\-option passes=1 \e
             \-\-option k_ladder=12,8,6,4 \-\-json run.json bench/c880.v
.EE
.RE
.PP
The same run on the driver, where the dedicated flags exist:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
export PYTHONHASHSEED=0
renesis \-\-optimize\-all \-\-k\-ladder 12,8,6,4 \-\-k\-ladder\-s 600 \e
        \-\-json run.json bench/c880.v
.EE
.RE
.PP
Check what a command line resolves to before committing the runtime:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
csrc/renesis \-\-option auto_bdd=true \-\-show\-options bench/c432.v
.EE
.RE
.PP
Price against a real workload rather than a uniform drive:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
csrc/renesis \-\-pi\-drive saif \-\-saif traces/c880.saif \e
             \-\-saif\-cycles 100000 bench/c880.v
.EE
.RE
.PP
Build the 2LAL pipeline buffer stages instead of only pricing them. This one
is driver\-only:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
renesis \-\-tech 2lal \-\-emit\-buffers bench/c432.v
.EE
.RE
.PP
Translate a netlist, with the round\-trip check on:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
csrc/renesis \-\-convert out.blif bench/c432.v
.EE
.RE
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Every non\-default option is echoed in the run header and recorded in the JSON
record, so a result always carries the conditions that produced it. Each
re\-synthesis pass reports its verdict, including the case where it found no
candidate that improved either energy table. Each ladder rung reports which K
lost and by how much.
.
